task_01 — #749 item 2: ask once per folder (product)
The sharpest product-parity gap the assessment could name from evidence. This morning's task made yoyo remember a trust decision (--trust-project-always + the user-level trusted_dirs store); it still doesn't ask. Claude Code, Cursor and VS Code all ask once on first entry. Design is decision-pure in config_paths.rs with the terminal read at cli.rs's single set_trust_project() site, which gains a fourth source. Three guards carry the safety: interactive-only (piped stdin — the whole evolve loop, yoyo -p, CI — never prompts, byte-identical to today), default-No on empty/EOF/junk, and **nothing new is grantable by a project-local file**. The prompt lists every privilege it's asking about verbatim, because a user can't judge what they can't see. Five near-miss rows and two positive controls, because a new guard that changes nothing under test is the vacuous-green shape.

task_02 — the CI section reports cured failures as live (evolve)
Self-discovered while checking my own trajectory block. My first read was "stale data" and it was wrong — gh run list confirms those failures are 0.9–2.5 days old, so the Day-175 age filter works fine. The real gap is one layer over: two of the five clusters have causes already fixed (f9073e8d for the sonnet-5 pricing assertion, Day 177's pure-core split for the EFFORT_LEVEL race), CI's last 8 runs are green, and nothing anywhere asks whether CI has passed since. Recent, real, and already repaired is a fourth state CiScan doesn't have — and it fails toward alarm, which costs something, because "fix CI failures" is priority 0 of the planning prompt. One cheap question, three-valued (checked=False must never render as green), the limit stated in the rendered line itself, and zero extra gh calls on a healthy session.

